WEBVTT
NOTE what-is-a-semiconductor-made-of - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.616
Silicon accounts for more than ninety
percent of semiconductor production by

00:00:04.616 --> 00:00:07.653
volume, according to SEMI International
Standards.

00:00:07.653 --> 00:00:12.134
That figure tells you the industry's
choice, not the physical requirement.

00:00:12.134 --> 00:00:15.040
A semiconductor is not defined by being
silicon.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.844
It is defined by having an electrical
conductivity you can control through

00:00:19.844 --> 00:00:22.960
temperature, light, or deliberate
contamination.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.798
The material itself sits between conductor
and insulator, and that middle ground is

00:00:27.798 --> 00:00:29.547
where the engineering happens.

00:00:29.547 --> 00:00:31.451
What is a semiconductor made of?

00:00:31.451 --> 00:00:34.721
Pure silicon at room temperature is a poor
conductor.

00:00:34.721 --> 00:00:39.040
Every silicon atom bonds to four neighbors
in a diamond cubic lattice.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.420
Those bonds lock electrons in place.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:46.710
Intrinsic silicon — the term for material
with no intentional impurities — has a

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.949
conductivity millions of times lower than
copper.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:51.578
The crystal is symmetric.

00:00:51.578 --> 00:00:56.920
Charge carriers exist only when thermal
energy breaks a bond, creating an electron

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.744
and a hole in matched pairs.

00:00:58.744 --> 00:01:01.560
That symmetry is the starting point, not
the goal.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.869
The engineering begins when you break it.

00:01:03.869 --> 00:01:09.292
You make silicon useful by adding
impurities at parts-per-million

00:01:09.292 --> 00:01:10.765
This is called doping.

00:01:10.765 --> 00:01:15.734
If you introduce phosphorus or arsenic —
elements from group fifteen of the

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:20.768
periodic table — each atom brings five
valence electrons into a lattice that

00:01:20.768 --> 00:01:21.629
expects four.

00:01:21.629 --> 00:01:26.415
The extra electron becomes a mobile
negative charge carrier.

00:01:26.415 --> 00:01:28.568
The material is now n-type.

00:01:28.568 --> 00:01:33.517
If you introduce boron or gallium from
group thirteen, each atom accepts an

00:01:33.517 --> 00:01:37.675
electron to complete its bonds, leaving a
mobile positive hole.

00:01:37.675 --> 00:01:41.897
The material is p-type. The conductivity
is no longer symmetric.

00:01:41.897 --> 00:01:47.374
You have engineered an imbalance, and that
imbalance is what transistors and diodes

00:01:47.374 --> 00:01:47.901
exploit.

00:01:47.901 --> 00:01:49.271
The ratio matters.

00:01:49.271 --> 00:01:54.901
One dopant atom per million silicon atoms
changes resistivity by orders of

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:55.661
magnitude.

00:01:55.661 --> 00:02:00.314
Semiconductor-grade silicon begins with
purity near one part per billion for

00:02:00.314 --> 00:02:03.987
electrically active impurities, according
to SEMI standards.

00:02:03.987 --> 00:02:09.155
That level is measured by resistivity
mapping and secondary ion mass

00:02:09.155 --> 00:02:14.290
The Czochralski process grows single
crystals from molten silicon in a quartz

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.424
crucible under inert atmosphere.

00:02:16.424 --> 00:02:21.350
Oxygen from the crucible dissolves into
the melt at parts-per-million levels.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:26.340
Carbon, metals, and dopants from previous
runs are removed by zone refining or

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.219
controlled segregation during
solidification.

00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:31.655
The purity target is not arbitrary.

00:02:31.655 --> 00:02:37.433
A single unintended atom per billion can
shift carrier concentration enough to ruin

00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:39.731
a transistor's threshold voltage.

00:02:39.731 --> 00:02:44.661
The measurement techniques have detection
limits in the parts-per-trillion range for

00:02:44.661 --> 00:02:49.473
some elements, but production monitoring
operates at parts-per-billion sensitivity

00:02:49.473 --> 00:02:53.229
because that is where electrical
properties become reproducible.

00:02:53.229 --> 00:02:55.963
Silicon is an elemental semiconductor.

00:02:55.963 --> 00:03:01.430
Gallium arsenide, gallium nitride, indium
phosphide, and silicon carbide are

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:06.896
compound semiconductors — crystals built
from two elements in stoichiometric

00:03:06.896 --> 00:03:07.400
ratios.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.333
Gallium arsenide has a bandgap near one
point four electronvolts compared to

00:03:12.333 --> 00:03:15.448
silicon's one point one, according to NIST
data.

00:03:15.448 --> 00:03:19.125
Gallium nitride reaches three point four
electronvolts.

00:03:19.125 --> 00:03:22.067
Silicon carbide sits near three point
three.

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:26.683
A wider bandgap allows operation at higher
voltages and temperatures.

00:03:26.683 --> 00:03:31.700
Gallium nitride transistors survive
junction temperatures above two hundred

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:32.771
degrees Celsius.

00:03:32.771 --> 00:03:37.374
Silicon carbide is used in power
electronics for electric vehicles because

00:03:37.374 --> 00:03:42.102
it switches faster and wastes less energy
as heat than silicon at equivalent

00:03:42.102 --> 00:03:42.600
ratings.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.986
The bandgap is not adjustable after
growth.

00:03:44.986 --> 00:03:47.427
You choose the compound for the
application.

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:51.936
The bandgap is the energy difference
between the valence band, where electrons

00:03:51.936 --> 00:03:55.347
are bound, and the conduction band, where
they move freely.

00:03:55.347 --> 00:04:00.015
A photon with energy greater than the
bandgap can promote an electron across

00:04:00.015 --> 00:04:00.568
that gap.

00:04:00.568 --> 00:04:05.362
Silicon's one point one electronvolt
bandgap corresponds to infrared light near

00:04:05.362 --> 00:04:07.485
one thousand nanometers wavelength.

00:04:07.485 --> 00:04:13.148
Gallium arsenide absorbs visible red.
Gallium nitride absorbs blue and

00:04:13.148 --> 00:04:18.128
This is why blue LEDs require gallium
nitride or indium gallium nitride —

00:04:18.128 --> 00:04:22.563
silicon's bandgap is too narrow to emit
blue photons efficiently.

00:04:22.563 --> 00:04:27.505
The material's composition sets the
bandgap, and the bandgap sets the optical

00:04:27.505 --> 00:04:28.083
response.

00:04:28.083 --> 00:04:32.963
You cannot tune a silicon photodetector to
see blue light. The physics forbids it.

00:04:32.963 --> 00:04:35.991
A semiconductor is not just a chemical
formula.

00:04:35.991 --> 00:04:38.440
It is a crystal with long-range order.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.480
Dislocations — line defects where the
lattice is misaligned — trap charge

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.827
carriers and create leakage paths.

00:04:45.827 --> 00:04:50.341
High-performance devices require
dislocation densities below ten thousand

00:04:50.341 --> 00:04:54.856
per square centimeter, measured by
etch-pit counting or X-ray topography.

00:04:54.856 --> 00:04:58.280
Grain boundaries between misoriented
crystals are worse.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:02.867
Polycrystalline silicon is used in solar
cells where cost matters more than

00:05:02.867 --> 00:05:07.514
efficiency, but microprocessors demand
single-crystal wafers with controlled

00:05:07.514 --> 00:05:08.248
orientation.

00:05:08.248 --> 00:05:13.597
The crystal is grown, sliced, polished,
and mapped for defects before any doping

00:05:13.597 --> 00:05:15.001
or patterning begins.

00:05:15.001 --> 00:05:20.349
A chemically perfect composition in a
defective crystal is electrically useless.

00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:23.829
'What is it made of' sounds like it wants
one element.

00:05:23.829 --> 00:05:28.598
Silicon is over ninety percent of
production, but the defining property is

00:05:28.598 --> 00:05:32.528
the impurity deliberately added to it, not
the base material.

00:05:32.528 --> 00:05:35.621
I answered the question underneath the
question.

00:05:35.621 --> 00:05:41.387
You are not limited to binary compounds.
Indium gallium arsenide is a ternary

00:05:41.387 --> 00:05:47.219
The composition is written as indium-x
gallium-one-minus-x arsenide, where x

00:05:47.219 --> 00:05:49.061
ranges from zero to one.

00:05:49.061 --> 00:05:52.406
At x equals zero you have gallium
arsenide.

00:05:52.406 --> 00:05:55.595
At x equals one you have indium arsenide.

00:05:55.595 --> 00:06:01.117
At intermediate values the bandgap varies
smoothly between zero point four and one

00:06:01.117 --> 00:06:02.800
point four electronvolts.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.420
This lets you design a photodetector for a
specific infrared wavelength by adjusting

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.291
the indium fraction during growth.

00:06:09.291 --> 00:06:12.857
Aluminum gallium nitride works the same
way for ultraviolet.

00:06:12.857 --> 00:06:17.552
The lattice constant also changes with
composition, which matters when you grow

00:06:17.552 --> 00:06:19.216
one alloy on top of another.

00:06:19.216 --> 00:06:24.043
Lattice mismatch creates strain and
defects unless the layers are thin or the

00:06:24.043 --> 00:06:28.117
mismatch is small. You are trading one
parameter against another.

00:06:28.117 --> 00:06:30.525
A transistor is not a bulk material.

00:06:30.525 --> 00:06:33.936
It is a stack of layers with engineered
interfaces.

00:06:33.936 --> 00:06:40.272
A silicon MOSFET has a p-type or n-type
channel, a silicon dioxide gate insulator,

00:06:40.272 --> 00:06:43.595
and heavily doped source and drain
regions.

00:06:43.595 --> 00:06:49.743
The oxide is grown by thermal oxidation at
nine hundred to eleven hundred degrees

00:06:49.743 --> 00:06:51.792
Celsius in oxygen or steam.

00:06:51.792 --> 00:06:56.081
The thickness is controlled to nanometers
by time and temperature.

00:06:56.081 --> 00:07:01.541
The interface between silicon and silicon
dioxide has trap states — energy levels in

00:07:01.541 --> 00:07:06.870
the bandgap caused by dangling bonds and
impurities — that are reduced by hydrogen

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:07.520
annealing.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:12.261
The carrier mobility in the channel
depends on interface roughness and charge.

00:07:12.261 --> 00:07:14.974
You are engineering boundaries, not bulk.

00:07:14.974 --> 00:07:18.944
The device physics happens where one
material meets another.

00:07:18.944 --> 00:07:23.604
Every high-temperature step risks
redistributing dopants you placed earlier.

00:07:23.604 --> 00:07:28.203
Phosphorus and boron diffuse through
silicon at rates that double every ten

00:07:28.203 --> 00:07:30.779
degrees Celsius near one thousand degrees.

00:07:30.779 --> 00:07:35.903
If you implant boron to form a shallow
junction and then oxidize at eleven

00:07:35.903 --> 00:07:38.880
hundred degrees, the boron profile
spreads.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.863
Process sequences are ordered by
decreasing temperature to minimize this

00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:44.901
redistribution.

00:07:44.901 --> 00:07:50.715
The highest-temperature steps — oxidation,
annealing, epitaxial growth — come first.

00:07:50.715 --> 00:07:54.248
Lower-temperature metallization and
passivation come last.

00:07:54.248 --> 00:07:59.061
The thermal budget is the cumulative time
at temperature the wafer experiences.

00:07:59.061 --> 00:08:02.564
Exceeding it blurs the doping profiles you
designed.

00:08:02.564 --> 00:08:05.461
The composition you engineer is not
static.

00:08:05.461 --> 00:08:08.560
It evolves with every subsequent process
step.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.938
Metal impurities — iron, copper, nickel —
diffuse through silicon at device

00:08:13.938 --> 00:08:15.659
processing temperatures.

00:08:15.659 --> 00:08:21.120
Even parts-per-trillion concentrations
degrade transistor performance by creating

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.155
mid-gap states that increase leakage
current.

00:08:24.155 --> 00:08:29.275
Gettering is the deliberate introduction
of defects or impurity sinks far from the

00:08:29.275 --> 00:08:32.523
active device region, usually at the wafer
backside.

00:08:32.523 --> 00:08:37.210
Phosphorus-doped backside layers or
mechanical damage create sites where

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:39.749
mobile metals preferentially segregate.

00:08:39.749 --> 00:08:43.611
During high-temperature anneals,
contaminants diffuse toward these

00:08:43.611 --> 00:08:45.835
gettering sites and are trapped there.

00:08:45.835 --> 00:08:49.027
The technique does not remove impurities
from the wafer.

00:08:49.027 --> 00:08:52.048
It moves them to a location where they do
not matter.

00:08:52.048 --> 00:08:57.043
The composition of the active region is
protected by engineering the composition

00:08:57.043 --> 00:08:58.667
of the sacrificial region.

00:08:58.667 --> 00:09:03.362
The numbers in this script trace to
measurement standards, not marketing.

00:09:03.362 --> 00:09:08.123
SEMI International Standards publish
semiconductor material specifications

00:09:08.123 --> 00:09:10.245
including purity and resistivity.

00:09:10.245 --> 00:09:15.385
NIST provides bandgap and lattice constant
data for elemental and compound

00:09:15.385 --> 00:09:16.427
semiconductors.

00:09:16.427 --> 00:09:21.122
ASM International documents crystal growth
and defect characterization methods.

00:09:21.122 --> 00:09:25.460
When you see a figure for dislocation
density or dopant concentration, it

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:29.680
reflects a measurement technique with
known precision and known limits.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.570
The composition of a semiconductor is not
a single answer.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:39.070
It is a controlled recipe with tolerances
in the parts-per-million range, verified

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:41.216
by multiple independent methods.

00:09:41.216 --> 00:09:45.841
The purity figures in this script
represent production targets, not

00:09:45.841 --> 00:09:47.291
thermodynamic limits.

00:09:47.291 --> 00:09:52.391
Published ranges for background oxygen in
Czochralski silicon span an order of

00:09:52.391 --> 00:09:55.072
magnitude depending on growth conditions.

00:09:55.072 --> 00:10:00.687
The exact impurity profile in a finished
device is proprietary and varies by

00:10:00.687 --> 00:10:01.648
manufacturer.

00:10:01.648 --> 00:10:06.593
What is certain is the requirement:
electrical properties must be reproducible

00:10:06.593 --> 00:10:11.728
lot to lot, and that requires composition
control far tighter than any other bulk

00:10:11.728 --> 00:10:16.419
material industry. The measurement exists.
The universal profile does not.

00:10:16.419 --> 00:10:21.757
A semiconductor is made of elements chosen
for their bandgap, doped with impurities

00:10:21.757 --> 00:10:26.966
at parts-per-million levels to control
carrier type, grown as a single crystal to

00:10:26.966 --> 00:10:32.304
minimize defects, and stacked in layers to
form interfaces where the device physics

00:10:32.304 --> 00:10:32.819
happens.

00:10:32.819 --> 00:10:37.923
The material is not the silicon. It is the
controlled asymmetry inside it.
